 "notes": "Watson and Schopp, SIR 2009-5167 v2.0 (May 2010), which revised the equations, Table 5, Table 6, the 1990 population densities and Appendix 2's R and W columns of the September 2009 v1.0; v1.0 is not used. One set of slopes (equations 7-13) on drainage area, storage + 1, 10-85 slope and 1990 population density + 1, with a regional constant KTR from Table 5 for each of 5 regions, so each region repeats the slopes and differs in its intercept (log10 KTR) and ranges, at 7 AEPs from 254 streamgages. Table 5 prints its columns OC, UP, GP, VR, IN, unlike Table 4. The 1 is stored as printed, as offset 1; NSS writes the same term as log10_plus1. Ranges from Table 4, and SEP in percent and equivalent years of record from Table 6. Table 6 also prints the standard error of estimate, 49, 49.4, 50.5, 52.6, 54.7, 57.1 and 63.9 percent from the 50 to the 0.2 percent AEP, which the schema has no field for. The report prints no covariance, model error variance or AVP, so intervals use the SEP. Table 7's drainage-area exponents for transfers from a gage on the same stream are GP 0.68, IN 0.66, OC 0.73, UP 0.58, VR 0.59, and are not used by the evaluator. Every coefficient, constant, range and SEP equals NSS. Appendix 2's regression flows reproduce at 249 of 254 gages from Table 3 within the rounding of what is printed. At 01401200, 01396660, 01405030, 01409280 and 01386500 they imply a drainage area other than Table 3's (3.82, 11.7, 44.7, 15.8 and 1.01 mi2), so those five are a fault in the printed inputs and not in the equations. Appendix 2's weighted flows are not equation 14 with Table 1's Ne and Table 6's E at many gages, and the Rahway example gets 6,330 ft3/s where Appendix 2 prints 6,350; weighted flows are not stored here. The report develops the equations on 1990 population density, and says StreamStats offers 2000 density in its place.",
